<!-- Not a v7 page: v7 had no directory library at all.  See BESM-6 NOTES. -->

# DIRECTORY(3)

## NAME

opendir, readdir, closedir, rewinddir, telldir, seekdir, dirfd - read a directory

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <dirent.h>

DIR *opendir(const char *name);

struct dirent *readdir(DIR *dirp);

int closedir(DIR *dirp);

void rewinddir(DIR *dirp);

long telldir(DIR *dirp);

void seekdir(DIR *dirp, long loc);

int dirfd(DIR *dirp);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Opendir* opens the directory named by *name* and returns a pointer to a directory stream,
positioned at the first entry. It returns NULL if the name cannot be opened, or is not a
directory, or if there is no memory for the stream.

*Readdir* returns a pointer to the next entry, or NULL at the end of the directory. The object
it points at has the following structure:

> ```c
> struct dirent {
>     ino_t d_ino;
>     int   d_namlen;
>     char  d_name[DIRSIZ + 1];
> };
> ```

**d_ino** is the entry's i-number and is never zero; **d_name** is the name, NUL-terminated;
**d_namlen** is its length, which *readdir* has already computed, so a caller need not call
strlen(3).

The structure is the stream's own and the next *readdir* on the same stream overwrites it,
exactly as getpwent(3) overwrites its own. A caller that wants to keep a name copies it out.

*Closedir* closes the stream, releases everything *opendir* allocated, and returns the result
of the underlying close(2).

*Telldir* returns a value naming the position *readdir* will read from next; *seekdir* returns
the stream to a position *telldir* reported earlier. *Rewinddir* returns it to the beginning.

*Dirfd* returns the descriptor the stream is holding, so that a caller may fstat(2) the
directory it is walking without opening it a second time. A caller must not close, read or seek
that descriptor: the stream's buffer and position would then describe a place in the file that
no longer exists.

