lib/libc/gen/readdir.c
lib/libc/gen/closedir.c
lib/libc/gen/rewinddir.c
lib/libc/gen/telldir.c
lib/libc/gen/seekdir.c
lib/libc/gen/dirfd.c
```

## SEE ALSO

dir(5), open(2), read(2), stat(2), ttyname(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Opendir* returns NULL and sets *errno:* **ENOTDIR** when the name is not a directory, and
otherwise whatever open(2) or malloc(3) set. *Readdir* returns NULL at the end of the directory
and on a read error alike, and does not distinguish them.

## BESM-6 NOTES

**This is not a v7 interface.** v7 had none at all: every caller opened the directory as a file
and read(2) raw **struct direct** records out of it, and eleven programs in *cmd/* each carried
their own copy of the same mistakes. 4.2BSD introduced the library and this is its shape, less
what has no meaning here. Every one of those eleven uses this library now; the programs that
still read a **struct direct** - fsck(1m), mkfs(1m), ncheck(1m), dcheck(1m) and pstat(1m) - take
it out of a block they read from the raw disk themselves, where there is no directory to open.

**struct dirent is not struct direct.** dir(5)'s **struct direct** is the format on the disk:
exactly four words, with **d_name** being **DIRSIZ** characters and **no room for a
terminator** when a name fills the field. **DIRSIZ is 18 here,** not v7's 14. *Readdir* copies
the name into a field one character longer and plants that terminator, which is the whole
reason for the library: a strlen(3) on the on-disk field runs into the next entry's **d_ino**.
A **struct dirent** is never read into and nothing about it is on any disk.

**readdir skips the empty slots.** unlink(2) empties a directory entry by writing **d_ino** = 0
back in place, so a hole sits in the middle of a live directory and every open-coded caller in
this tree had to test for it by hand. *Readdir* does that test once, for all of them.

**telldir returns the plain byte offset,** and there is no machinery behind it. 4.2BSD returns
an index into a list of seek points that *closedir* must free, because a BSD directory entry is
variable-length and an offset alone cannot name one. Every entry here is exactly **DIRENTSZ**
(24) bytes, so the offset is the entry. *Seekdir* rounds its argument down to a whole entry.

**The read buffer is sized from the directory** - as many entries as it holds when it is
opened, capped at **DIRPB** (128), which is exactly one filesystem block, so a full read is one
buffer-cache block and no entry straddles the buffer. A fixed one-block buffer would be 512
words for a */dev* of twenty-five entries, and **_NFILE** is 20: seventeen directories open at
once would be 8,704 words of a 28,672-word address space.

**opendir refuses a plain file,** which neither v7 nor 4.2BSD had to do. Read(2) on a regular
file succeeds here, so without the test *readdir* would hand back file contents reinterpreted
as entries - silently, there being no field in a directory entry that can be checked for sense.

**lib/test/dirt is the test, and it runs on the disk image only.** *b6sim* serves every system
call from the host, where open(2) and fstat(2) on a directory both **succeed** and only read(2)
refuses. So under the simulator *opendir* returns a perfectly good stream and the first
*readdir* returns NULL: every directory looks empty, and looks it in exactly the way an empty
one does. No expectation file can tell those apart, which is why the test is image-only and why
a *b6sim* run of anything that walks a tree proves nothing about the walk.
